"""Chapter 18 deck notes, version 4.0.1 (SEN0414, Lineage13): the notes the reader gets with the slides (4.0.1 over 4.0.0: three sentences corrected after the link check of ST13_Measure_Links: the links of slides 28 and 42 lead to an article of heise and to a post by Eli Bendersky, and slide 35 compares with slide 5), one for every slide, written in full sentences for a student who reads the deck alone.
Each note explains the short wording of its slide, names what the run printed, and points to a related slide; none gives a direction to a presenter and none uses an everyday comparison.
RESULT_SAYS holds the first result balloon of every code slide (what the run showed); the second balloon is TAKEAWAY of the content file. Slide numbers are those of PLAN."""
__version__ = "4.0.1"

NOTES = {
"title": "This chapter of the book, CSV, JSON and XML files, shows three ways to keep data as plain text and the standard library calls that read and write each one. CSV keeps a table, JSON keeps records of named values, and XML keeps a tree of elements. Every example builds its own small data in memory or in a temporary folder, so nothing depends on the book's files. The deck has twenty-seven code examples that run on the chapter page, in the same order, in the Slide code tab. Slide 2 shows the six parts of the chapter.",
"route": "The route has six parts, and each row gives the slides of the part, how many code examples it holds and what you will run. Part 1 reads CSV files, part 2 writes them, part 3 is Project 13, part 4 is JSON, part 5 is XML, and part 6 is the practice program that turns Excel sheets into CSV files. The last row covers the tools table, the list of what differs from the book today, the summary, the practice questions and the links. Use the slide ranges to find one format quickly. Slide 3 states the two questions that frame the chapter.",
"questions": "Two questions frame the chapter, and the answers come back at the end. The first asks how to read and write a table, a record and a tree with the standard library, and what each format loses on the way; slide 49 answers it in a table. The second asks which printed outputs, programs and statements of the book differ in Python today; slide 50 answers it from the chapter research. Keep both in mind while reading the code: what did this call keep, and what did it change? Part 1 starts on slide 4 with reading CSV files.",
"divider1": "Part 1 is about reading CSV files. A CSV file is records on lines, and each record is a list of fields. The four cards are the ideas of the part: the reader cuts the fields of a line, a list keeps every row because a reader runs only once, the line number counts lines and not records, and next takes a header row off the reader. Slides 7 to 10 show each of them in code. Splitting a line on commas looks enough, and slide 7 shows the case where it is not. Part 2 then writes CSV files.",
"A1": "A CSV file holds one record on each line, and commas separate the fields of a record. The csv module reads and writes that format, including a quoted field that contains a comma. Reading with csv.reader cuts each line into a list of fields, a for loop takes one row at a time so a large file need not fit in memory, and writing with csv.writer turns lists back into lines and quotes a field when it needs quoting. Slide 6 tells where the format came from, and slide 7 shows why a plain split on commas is not enough.",
"ibm": "The photograph shows an IBM System/360 console and tape drives in a museum; this is the machine family of OS/360, not the compiler itself. In 1972 the Fortran compiler of these mainframes already read values separated by commas, and the name CSV was in use by 1983. Free-form input saved counting columns, but an unquoted string could hold no comma. Quotes were the later answer, and csv.reader keeps a comma inside quotes in one field. The link leads to the Wikipedia page on comma-separated values. Slide 7 shows that problem in code.",
"csv_split": "The printed lines show that split gives four pieces, because it cuts at the comma inside the quotes, while reader gives the three fields that were meant. The line holds a name, an age and a city with a comma in it, so the third field is wrapped in double quotes. That is why the csv module exists: a quoted field may contain a comma. The last printed line shows two records of two fields, a file as a list of records and a record as a list of fields. Slide 8 reads a whole file with reader and list.",
"reader_list": "A small stand-in file is written to a temporary folder, opened, passed to csv.reader, and turned into a list of rows. The printed result shows the rows, each a list of strings, and then one value picked by row and column. The last printed word is str: even the age 30 comes back as text, so you convert numbers yourself when you need them. Compare slide 30, where JSON keeps a number as a number. Slide 9 reads the same kind of data with a for loop and shows what the reader keeps.",
"for_loop": "The loop prints each row with its line number, and the numbers jump from one to three because one record has a line break inside quotes. The line number counts lines of the file, not records. The last two printed lines show that a second pass over the reader gives an empty list and that the reader has no length. A reader can be used once, so call list when you need to keep the rows, as slide 8 did. The loop itself holds only one row in memory. Slide 10 uses next to skip a header row and shows where the line number test fails.",
"next_header": "The first two printed lines show that next takes the header off the reader and what remains is the data without it. The last line shows a header that spans two lines because it holds a line break in quotes. The book's test, line number equal to one, fails here: the header record ends on line two, so the header is kept as data. Taking the first row with next does not depend on how many lines it spans, so it is the safer habit. Slide 11 turns around and starts part 2, writing CSV files.",
"divider2": "Part 2 is about writing CSV files. The four cards are the ideas of the part: csv.writer writes lists and DictWriter writes dictionaries, a delimiter other than the comma such as a tab is just a setting, the newline argument stops Windows from doubling the line ends, and the encoding matters because files that Excel saves as UTF-8 start with a byte order mark. Slides 14 to 19 hold one example for each of these. Part 1 read what part 2 writes, so the same rows travel in both directions. Slide 12 explains the terms.",
"A2": "A header row names the columns and holds no data record. A tab as the delimiter changes only the character between the fields; the records stay the same. A file written on Windows without newline set to the empty string gets a carriage return twice at the end of every record, so a spreadsheet shows a blank line after each one; this is the double-spaced file of Figure 18-1 in the book, and slide 16 reproduces it. The book's file exampleWithHeader3.csv is the practice file for skipping a header, as slide 10 did. Slide 13 tells how CSV was finally written down.",
"rfc": "The photograph shows a meeting room of the Internet Engineering Task Force, the body behind the RFC series; it does not show the author. In October 2005, after more than thirty years of CSV files, Yakov Shafranovich wrote RFC 4180, which documents the common format and registers the type text/csv. The memo is informational and not a standard, which is why programs still read CSV in many ways. The last paragraph says that records end in carriage return and line feed. Slide 14 shows that ending in bytes. The link leads to the RFC.",
"writer_basics": "The last printed line shows the bytes: every record ends with a carriage return and a line feed, the CRLF of the RFC on slide 13. The first lines show how many characters each writerow call wrote. The second record in the bytes shows that the field with a comma is wrapped in double quotes, while numbers and None have become plain text, with None as an empty field. A StringIO stands in for the open file with newline set to the empty string. Slide 15 changes the delimiter and the line terminator.",
"tabs": "The bytes show that the fields are separated by a tab and the records end in a plain line feed. Two arguments did that: delimiter changes the character between fields, and lineterminator changes what ends a record. Words with spaces need no quotes when the delimiter is a tab. The list on the last line shows that reading the bytes back with the same delimiter returns the rows that were written. The rule is to write and read with the same settings. Slide 16 shows the double-spaced file and the argument that prevents it.",
"newline_blank": "Two printed lines are compared. The first starts with an empty string and shows the bytes a, b, carriage return, line feed: two clean records. The second, from the Windows stand-in, shows carriage return, carriage return, line feed, so a reader finds an empty record after every row. That is the double spacing of the book's figure. A text wrapper asked for CRLF plays Windows here, so this is a stand-in and not a real Windows run. The fix is newline set to the empty string when the file is opened. Slide 17 writes one row with six quoting constants.",
"quoting": "The same row is written six times, once with each quoting constant. The default, minimal, quotes only the field with the comma. All quotes every field, non-numeric leaves numbers bare, and none escapes the comma with a backslash instead of quoting it. The two newer constants, strings and not-null, treat None and the empty string differently, so compare their last two fields. For a file that another program must read, choose the constant that program expects. Slide 18 shows the encoding that the chapter never names, and a three-byte mark at the start of a file.",
"encoding_bom": "The first printed line shows three bytes, the byte order mark, before the first letter of the file, because Excel's CSV UTF-8 option writes them. Decoded as plain utf-8 the mark stays as an invisible first character, and the last line shows it glued to the first key, so a lookup of Name would raise a KeyError. The codec utf-8-sig removes it. The habit to keep is to pass an encoding to open every time. Slide 19 shows DictReader and DictWriter, which give every row its column names. Slide 33 meets the same mark in JSON.",
"dict_rw": "The first two printed dictionaries show that DictReader used the first row as keys, and the short row for Bob got None for the missing value. The last line shows that DictWriter wrote the header from its field names and filled a missing key with an empty field, so the row of Eve ends in a comma. Dictionaries make rows readable by name instead of by position, which survives a reordered column. Slide 20 starts part 3, Project 13, which removes the header row from every CSV file in a folder.",
"divider3": "Part 3 is Project 13, removing the header row. The four cards are three steps and a pointer to similar programs. Step 1 loops through the files of a folder, step 2 reads a file and skips its header row, and step 3 writes the other rows to a new file. Slides 22 to 24 give each step its own small example, and slide 25 shows how far the same plan reaches. Step 2 uses next, which slide 10 introduced. Slide 21 explains the terms of the project.",
"A3": "Project 13 reads every CSV file of a folder and writes a copy of each without its header row. The three steps are to go through the names in a folder and keep those that end in .csv, to read one file and leave out its first row, and to write the remaining rows to a file of the same name in another folder. The originals stay untouched, because the program writes new files. Totals, merges and filters follow the same plan: loop, read, then do something with each row. Slide 22 shows step 1.",
"p13_loop": "Only a.csv and b.csv are announced. Four empty files stand in for the project's folder, one of them a text file and one with a capital extension, and the loop skips both because the test on the ending is case sensitive. So C.CSV is skipped, which surprises people; lowering the name before testing it makes capitals count. Sorting the names first makes the order the same on every machine. Slide 23 shows step 2, which reads a file and skips its first row.",
"p13_read": "The first printed line shows that the header is gone and one data row is left. The second line shows an empty file, where next returned None instead of raising an error. That is the reason for the second argument of next: without it, an empty file would stop the whole program with StopIteration. A file with only a header gives an empty list, which is a correct answer. Compare slide 10, where next was used without a default. Slide 24 shows step 3, which writes the remaining rows to a new file.",
"p13_write": "The two printed lines show the line count of each file before and after, three to two and two to one. Every file is exactly one line shorter, which is the proof that only the header row was removed. The code skips the header with next, keeps the other rows in a list, and writes them with writerows to a file of the same name in a new folder. Checking the counts is more reliable than looking at the files. Slide 25 shows a similar program and how far the same plan reaches.",
"col_total": "The printed dictionary holds one running total for each region: North 180, South 80 and East 40. The values of a CSV file are text, so int converts them before adding, and the dictionary keeps one total per region. The plan is the same as Project 13: loop, read, then do something with each row, and only the last step differs. That is why small CSV programs are quick to write. Slide 26 moves from tables to records and starts part 4, JSON.",
"divider4": "Part 4 is about JSON. The four cards are loads, dumps, value types and strict syntax. JSON has six kinds of value: strings, numbers, booleans, null, lists and dictionaries. loads reads JSON text into Python values, dumps writes Python values as JSON text, and a round trip changes some of them, for example tuples become lists. The syntax is strict, and a trailing comma is an error. Slide 29 shows the same small record in all three formats first. Slide 27 explains the terms.",
"A4": "JSON writes a record as named values: every value has a name, and a list holds values that repeat. json.loads turns JSON text into Python dictionaries, lists and values, and json.dumps turns Python values into JSON text, where a tuple comes back as a list. The json module refuses badly written text instead of guessing. One record can be a CSV line, JSON or XML, and each format keeps a different amount of structure; slide 29 compares them. Slide 28 tells where the name Ajax came from.",
"ajax": "On 18 February 2005 Jesse James Garrett named Ajax in an essay on the blog of his firm, Adaptive Path, with Google Suggest and Google Maps as his examples. The name is short for Asynchronous JavaScript and XML. The portrait shows Garrett. The last paragraph notes that XML is the X of Ajax, while this chapter reads the plain text, in JSON and XML, that a browser and a server pass to each other. The link leads to an article of heise that looks back on Ajax twenty years later. Slide 29 writes one record in all three formats.",
"formats_compare": "The same two people are written in CSV, JSON and XML, and the printed counts are 28, 58 and 111 characters. CSV is the smallest because it names the columns once. JSON repeats the keys for every person, and XML repeats opening and closing tags, so both carry their structure in every record. That is the price of structure. Choose the format by what the reader needs, not by habit; slide 49 lists what each format keeps and loses. Slide 30 reads JSON text and shows the six types of value.",
"json_loads": "loads read the text and returned a dictionary with a list inside. The translations are shown: null became None and true became True. The list of types on the last line shows one type for each value: str, int, NoneType, bool and list. The numbers kept their kind, so 30 is an integer here and not text, unlike the CSV values on slide 8. Slide 31 goes the other way with dumps, and the round trip will not return the original.",
"json_dumps": "The first printed line is the JSON text: the tuple became a list, the number key one became the text one, and the accented letter in Istanbul was escaped. The second line is the value read back, which is not the original, because the tuple is now a list and the key is a string. The third line says False: the round trip changed the data. The last lines show indent, which spreads the text over several lines for people. Slide 32 shows what json.loads refuses.",
"json_strict": "One printed line is given for each text that loads was given. A trailing comma, single quotes, Python's None and an empty text are all refused, each with a message and the column where the parser stopped. The line for NaN shows that it is accepted, although JSON has no NaN. That is a known gap between the standard and the module, so do not rely on it for data from other people. Compare slide 38, where XML refuses broken text with ParseError. Slide 33 works with files and meets the byte order mark again.",
"json_files": "The first line shows the bytes of the file as stored, with the accented letter as two bytes, because ensure_ascii was turned off. The True shows that load read the file back and this time the value equals the original, because it holds only types that survive. The last line shows the byte order mark: bytes that begin with it are accepted, but a string that begins with it is refused; slide 18 showed the same mark in a CSV file. Slide 34 leaves tables and records and turns to trees, with XML.",
"divider5": "Part 5 is about XML. The four cards are ElementTree, find, xmltodict and defusedxml. XML is a tree of elements, and ElementTree is the standard library's way to read and write that tree. Text is always a string or None. xmltodict turns a document into a dictionary, which is quick to read but easy to misread, and XML that comes from someone else needs defusedxml. The part has one explanation slide, three stories and six code slides, and slides 37 to 41 and 43 hold the code. Slide 35 explains the terms.",
"A5": "XML is a tree: every element has a name, may hold text and attributes, and may hold child elements. ElementTree parses the text into a tree whose elements you reach with find and findall, and Element and SubElement build a tree that tostring turns into XML text. xmltodict reads XML into a dictionary of strings and writes it back. The tags must match, and that strictness is a theme of this part. Compare slide 5, where a CSV file is plain lines of fields. Slide 36 tells how XML 1.0 was published.",
"xml": "XML 1.0 became a W3C Recommendation on 10 February 1998, after work that began in mid-1996, and Jon Bosak recruited collaborators who knew both SGML and the web. The portrait shows Bosak. The last paragraph explains the code that follows: the rules are strict on purpose, with one root and matching tags and case, and ElementTree raises ParseError when they break. A strict language is easy for a program to check. The link leads to the Wikipedia page. Slide 37 reads an XML document with ElementTree.",
"et_read": "The printed lines go in order. The root is a person with five children: the name, the age, the car and two phones. The name text is Alice Doe, and the empty car element has the text None. The two phone attributes are read with get, mobile and work. The last line shows str and 31: text is always a string, so convert numbers yourself, as with CSV on slide 8. Looping over an element gives its children, and findall collects every match. Slide 38 shows how strict the parser is with whitespace and broken XML.",
"et_traps": "The first printed line shows that the line break and spaces between the tags are kept, as the text of the parent and the tail of the child, so whitespace is data. The three error lines show that a closing tag that does not match, a second root and a capital letter in the wrong place all raise ParseError, each with a line and a column. XML is strict, and a program can rely on that. Compare slide 32, where json.loads refuses broken JSON. Slide 39 writes a tree with Element, SubElement and tostring.",
"et_write": "The first printed line is one long line of XML, in which the less-than sign and the ampersand of the note were escaped automatically. The indented version below it shows indent adding line breaks and spaces for people to read. Element makes the root and SubElement adds each child. Writing to a file is one more call, and xml_declaration adds the first line that starts an XML file. Compare slide 31, where json.dumps wrote JSON text from Python values. Slide 40 covers namespaces, where names are not what they seem.",
"et_nil": "The first printed dictionary shows the attribute nil under its full name, with the web address in braces, and ElementTree did not interpret it. The next line shows that the text is simply None. The prefix xsi is only a short name for an address declared in the document. The last line shows that an element in a namespace is written with the address in braces, so i and a colon i are different elements. Slide 41 reads the same kind of document as a dictionary with xmltodict.",
"xmltodict_rt": "The first printed dictionary shows that the attribute of phone has an at sign in front of its key, and its text sits under the key hash text. The next two lines compare one b child, which gives a string, with two, which give a list, so a program must expect both. The last lines show unparse writing the dictionary back as XML, and the round trip returns the same dictionary, with every value a string. Compare slide 37, where ElementTree gave elements. Slide 42 tells the story of the library behind ElementTree.",
"pylib": "The picture is the history comment at the top of Fredrik Lundh's ElementTree file, drawn as text, because no free portrait of him was found. Lundh created ElementTree on 20 October 2001, and it was added to the Python project on 12 December 2005. He kept the copyright and later handed its upkeep to the Python developers. The last paragraph notes that you write import xml.etree.ElementTree as ET, and that a module has a person behind it. The link leads to a post by Eli Bendersky on improving ElementTree for Python 3.3. Slide 43 shows what happens when XML comes from someone else.",
"xml_security": "The two printed lines show that the standard parser expands the entities inside the document and returns 30 characters from a few short lines, while defusedxml refuses the entities at once. Entity b stands for ten copies of entity a, so each further level multiplies the size by ten, and the large case is under 1 KB and expands to almost 3 GB. This is a tiny cousin of the billion laughs attack. XML from someone else should never be parsed without checking the parser. Slide 44 tells the story of that attack.",
"laughs": "The picture shows server racks in a data centre; it shows the kind of machine such a file can exhaust and does not show an attack. A document under 1 KB can have entities that expand to a billion copies of lol, almost 3 gigabytes. The attack was first reported as early as 2002 and widely addressed from 2008. The last paragraph ties it to the code: defusedxml refuses entities, as slide 43 showed. The link leads to the Wikipedia page on the billion laughs attack. Slide 45 starts part 6, the practice program that turns Excel into CSV.",
"divider6": "Part 6 is the practice program, Excel to CSV. The four cards are openpyxl, sheets, iter rows and the final check. The book's skeleton is an outline with the steps named, and the two examples finish it. A workbook holds sheets, a sheet holds rows, and the program writes one CSV file for each sheet; then it lists the files and reads them back to check. Slide 47 builds the workbook, and slide 48 converts it. Slide 46 explains the terms.",
"A6": "A converter reads a workbook and writes one CSV file for each of its sheets. openpyxl reads and writes Excel workbooks without Excel, and the book's example3.xlsx and excelSpreadsheets.zip are the practice files. shelve is a different tool: it stores Python objects in a file by name, and only Python can read that file, so it is not a text format like the three of this chapter. One CSV file per sheet is needed because a CSV file has no place for a second sheet. Slide 47 builds a workbook for the converter.",
"xl_make": "The printed line shows that the workbook has two sheets, Sales and Costs, and that a cell read back gives the number 120. The code made a new workbook with one sheet, appended rows after the last one, added a second sheet with create_sheet, saved into memory and loaded it again. The numbers stayed numbers, which a CSV file cannot promise; compare slide 8. This workbook stands in for the book's spreadsheet files. Slide 48 converts it: the loop goes over the sheets and writes the CSV files.",
"xl_convert": "The two printed lines show book-Sales.csv with two rows and book-Costs.csv with one. The loop goes over the sheets and writes a file for each, named after the workbook and the sheet, and iter_rows with values only gives plain values to write. The check at the end lists the files and reads them back, so the program proves its own result, as Project 13 did on slide 24. Slide 49 gathers the calls of the whole chapter in one table.",
"tools": "The table answers the first question of slide 3. Each row is a call of the chapter, with what it does, what to know about it, and the slide that shows it. The third column holds the traps, such as opening reader files with newline set to the empty string, and every CSV value being text. Reads change nothing, and writes make new text or a new file. The table also shows what each format loses: CSV has no types, JSON turns a tuple into a list, and XML keeps only strings. Slide 50 lists what differs from the book today.",
"current": "The left column holds what the book says, the middle column what is true today, and the last the slide that shows it. This slide answers the second question of slide 3. For example, the book advises opening reader files in binary mode, while today they are opened in text mode with newline set to the empty string, and a binary reader raises an error. The research behind the table is dated and names its versions. Run the book's lines first and compare them with these rows. Slide 51 is the summary.",
"summary": "The summary keeps five points to remember, and each can be traced to a slide. A CSV file is lines of fields, split by csv.reader, with newline set right when the file is opened. JSON has six value types and a strict syntax, and a round trip changes tuples. XML is a tree and all its text is strings. XML from someone else needs defusedxml. A converter writes one CSV file for each sheet. The chart counts the code examples in each part. Slide 52 turns these points into practice questions.",
"practice": "The practice questions can be answered in a few lines, and each can be checked by running code on the chapter page. The first asks what split and csv.reader return for a line with a quoted comma; slide 7 shows it. The second asks why the line number test fails for a header with a line break; slide 10 shows it. The third and fourth ask about the types JSON gives and the text and tail of an XML element, and the fifth asks for the steps of a converter, which slides 47 and 48 give. Predict first, then run.",
"resources": "The resources are the chapter of the book, the documentation of the csv, json and xml modules, the RFC, and the pages behind the stories of this chapter. Every link was checked when the chapter was prepared. Pick one link and read it before the next class. This is the last slide of the chapter, and the same examples wait in the Slide code tab of the page. See also slide 50 for what differs from the book today.",
}

